        <div class="guide-content" itemprop="articleBody">
            <section class="prose-section" id="daily-changes">
                <h2>Why body weight changes from day to day</h2>
                <p>A scale measures total body mass at that moment. It does not separately identify a change in body fat. Fluid intake and loss, food and drink still being digested, carbohydrate stores, sodium intake, clothing, and the time of measurement can all affect an individual reading.</p>
                <p>That is why two consecutive mornings can produce different numbers even when the longer-term direction has not meaningfully changed. The daily entry remains useful evidence; it just should not be asked to tell the entire story by itself.</p>
                <div class="guide-principle">
                    <strong>Daily readings are observations.</strong>
                    <span>The weekly average is the period-level summary WeekScale uses for comparison.</span>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="prose-section" id="calculation">
                <h2>How a weekly weight average is calculated</h2>
                <p>Add the recorded weights inside the calendar week, then divide by the number of recorded days. Missing days are not treated as zero.</p>
                <div class="calculation-card" aria-label="Example weekly average calculation">
                    <p><span>Recorded weights</span><strong>80.4 + 80.1 + 80.3 + 80.0 + 79.9 kg</strong></p>
                    <p><span>Number of entries</span><strong>5</strong></p>
                    <p class="calculation-result"><span>Weekly average</span><strong>80.14 kg</strong></p>
                </div>
                <p>WeekScale also shows the minimum, maximum, date range, number of entries, and change from the most recent earlier week that contains data.</p>
                <p>WeekScale groups readings into fixed calendar weeks. For the reasoning behind that choice, see <a class="inline-link" href="/why-calendar-weeks">why WeekScale uses calendar weeks</a>.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="prose-section" id="reliability">
                <h2>More readings make the average more representative</h2>
                <p>An average based on one reading technically exists, but it says little more than that one reading. WeekScale's Reliability Score reports how many of the seven calendar days contain an entry: one day is 14%, five days is 71%, and seven days is 100%.</p>
                <p>The score is not a judgment and it does not claim medical certainty. It is a visible reminder of how complete the period is. Weeks with five or more entries receive the app's reliable-week treatment.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="prose-section" id="consistent-weighing">
                <h2>Consistency improves comparability</h2>
                <p>If you choose to weigh regularly, similar conditions make readings easier to compare: roughly the same time of day, similar clothing, the same scale, and a consistent routine. WeekScale records what you enter; it does not attempt to &ldquo;correct&rdquo; a reading with an opaque model.</p>
                <p>Hydration, nutrition, activity, sleep, medication, health conditions, and many other factors can affect body weight or how it should be interpreted. For general health guidance, use reputable sources such as the <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/better-health/lose-weight/" rel="external">NHS Better Health guidance</a>, and speak with a qualified healthcare professional about personal concerns.</p>
                <aside class="medical-note">
                    <strong>WeekScale is a tracking tool, not medical advice.</strong>
                    <p>It calculates summaries from values you provide. It does not diagnose conditions, define a healthy weight, or recommend a treatment or weight-loss target.</p>
                </aside>
            </section>

